  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Gossypium barbadense」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
      "revision": 1378541477,
      "text": "Gossypium barbadense is one of several species of cotton. It is in the mallow family. It has been cultivated since antiquity, but has been especially prized since a form with particularly long fibers was developed in the 19th century. Other names associated with this species include Sea Island, Egyptian, Pima, and extra-long staple (ELS) cotton."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Gossypium barbadense」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gossypium_barbadense#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
        "revision": 1378541477,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The species accounts for about 5% of the world's cotton production.\nCertain regions specialize in G. barbadense. One reason is to prevent different species of cotton from hybridizing with each other. If a field of G. barbadense is too close to a field of a different species, the result is generally poor quality of the fiber.\nIn historical times, G. barbadense was mostly a dooryard crop and treated as a perennial. Individual households would maintain one or a few bushes for years at a time. This form of cultivation continues to a small extent to the present. However, nearly all cotton production, G. barbadense or otherwise, are now row crops and treated as an annual. Plants are grown from seed each year.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
